## Characterization of a Plastid Genome: *Dendrobium officinale*

## Introduction

Plastid genomes are useful for studying plant evolution, species identification, phylogenetic relationships, and molecular markers. In this activity, the complete chloroplast genome of *Dendrobium officinale* was characterized using information from the NCBI RefSeq database and sequence statistics generated in Galaxy. The plastid genome was also examined for its gene content, RNA-related features, structural organization, and other notable characteristics.

The selected organism was *Dendrobium officinale*, a member of the family Orchidaceae. Its complete chloroplast genome is available in NCBI RefSeq under accession **NC_024019.1**. The genome is described by NCBI as a complete chloroplast genome and has a length of **152,221 bp**.

## 1. Genome Identification and Source

The organism analyzed in this activity was *Dendrobium officinale* (family Orchidaceae). The selected sequence was **NC_024019.1**, described in NCBI as "*Dendrobium officinale* chloroplast, complete genome." The genome is classified as a plastid/chloroplast genome and has a circular topology.

The NCBI record indicates that the sequence is full length. The sequence contains **152,221 bp** and represents one complete sequence record. The reference associated with the genome is Luo et al. (2014).

## 2. Evidence That the Genome Is Complete

Several observations support that the sequence represents a complete plastid genome. First, the NCBI record identifies the sequence as a **complete genome** and indicates **full length**. Second, the sequence has a single complete record with a length of 152,221 bp.

The Galaxy FASTA Statistics analysis also supported the completeness of the sequence. The analysis produced one sequence with a length of 152,221 bp. The scaffold and contig N50 were both 152,221 bp, while L50 and L90 were both 1. The analysis also detected **zero gaps** and zero ambiguous N bases.

These results indicate that the sequence used for the analysis is a single complete, gap-free plastid sequence.

## 3. Genome Size, Topology, and Major Regions

The *Dendrobium officinale* chloroplast genome is **152,221 bp** long and has a **circular** topology.

The plastid genome is organized into the typical major regions of a chloroplast genome: a large single-copy region (LSC), a small single-copy region (SSC), and two inverted repeat regions (IRa and IRb). The two inverted repeat regions occur between the single-copy regions.

The chloroplast genome map generated using OGDRAW shows the LSC region, SSC region, IRa, and IRb. The map also displays genes and their transcriptional directions.

## 4. Protein-Coding Genes

The NCBI annotation contains numerous protein-coding genes. Examples include **psbA**, **matK**, and **rps16**.

The **psbA** gene is associated with photosystem II, while **matK** is located within the trnK intron and is a commonly examined plastid gene in plant studies. The annotated genome contains many additional genes involved in photosynthesis, gene expression, and other plastid functions.

The NCBI annotation contains **76 CDS features**. These counts represent annotated sequence features and should not necessarily be interpreted as the number of unique biological genes.

## 5. Gene Organization

The genes of the *D. officinale* chloroplast genome are distributed throughout the LSC, SSC, and IR regions. The OGDRAW visualization makes this organization easier to observe because the genes are arranged around the circular genome according to their genomic positions.

The two IR regions contain duplicated portions of the genome. The rRNA genes are particularly associated with the IR regions. Gene arrows on the map indicate the direction of transcription.

Luo et al. (2014) reported that the *D. officinale* chloroplast genome contained **110 different genes**, with 91 single-copy genes and 19 duplicated genes. The authors also found that the gene content was relatively conserved compared with other photosynthetic orchids.

## 6. Important RNA and RNA-Processing Features

The *Dendrobium officinale* chloroplast genome contains rRNA and tRNA genes involved in gene expression and protein synthesis. Luo et al. (2014) reported 4 rRNA genes and 30 tRNA genes in the chloroplast genome.

Examples of annotated rRNA genes include **rrn5, rrn4.5, rrn16, and rrn23**. Examples of tRNA genes include **trnK-UUU** and **trnQ-UUG**.

RNA processing is also represented by the **trnK intron**, which contains the **matK** gene. The NCBI annotation also identifies trans-splicing associated with **rps12**.

The NCBI annotation also shows intron-containing genes such as **atpF** and **ycf3**, whose coding sequences are annotated as joined regions.

Luo et al. (2014) also reported RNA-editing sites in chloroplast genes, including an RNA-editing event involving the initiation codon of **rpl2**.

## 7. Pseudogenes, Duplications, and Other Unusual Features

The chloroplast genome shows several features that are useful for understanding plastid genome evolution. Luo et al. (2014) reported 110 different genes, with 19 genes occurring in duplicated copies. This duplication is associated with the organization of the inverted repeat regions.

The authors also observed differences in the IR/SSC boundaries and in the presence of **ndh** genes among orchid chloroplast genomes. These differences demonstrate that although orchid plastid genomes are relatively conserved, structural variation can occur.

RNA editing is another notable feature. In *D. officinale*, RNA-editing sites were predicted in several genes, including an editing event involving the initiation codon of **rpl2**.

The NCBI annotation of NC_024019.1 also contains annotated pseudogene entries. These annotations were considered as part of the characterization of the genome. However, pseudogene observations from other orchid species should not automatically be assigned to *D. officinale*.

## 8. GC Content and Sequence Observations

The Galaxy FASTA Statistics analysis gave an overall GC content of **37.47%** for the *D. officinale* chloroplast genome.

The sequence consisted of one sequence record with a total length of 152,221 bp. The analysis showed:

- Number of sequences: **1**
- Sequence length: **152,221 bp**
- GC content: **37.47%**
- N50: **152,221 bp**
- L50: **1**
- N90: **152,221 bp**
- L90: **1**
- Number of gaps: **0**
- Number of N bases: **0**
- Minimum length: **152,221 bp**
- Maximum length: **152,221 bp**

The OGDRAW map also showed that GC content is not completely uniform throughout the plastid genome. The genome organization into LSC, SSC, and IR regions provides another important structural feature visible in the map.

## 9. Plastid and Mitochondrial Genome Comparison

The plastid and mitochondrial genomes are both organellar genomes and both contain genes involved in essential cellular processes. Both can contain protein-coding genes, tRNA genes, and rRNA genes. RNA editing has also been reported in both types of organellar genomes.

For comparison, a mitochondrial sequence from *D. officinale* was examined in NCBI: **OR413912.1**, described as "*Dendrobium officinale* chromosome 1 mitochondrion, complete sequence." This sequence is **65,398 bp** long and circular. Importantly, this accession represents **mitochondrial chromosome 1**, not the complete mitochondrial genome.

The plastid genome analyzed in this activity is a single circular 152,221-bp sequence, whereas *Dendrobium* mitochondrial genomes can contain multiple chromosomes and show greater structural variation. Wang et al. (2024) described substantial structural and organizational variation among *Dendrobium* mitochondrial genomes, including differences in synteny, intercellular gene transfer, repeats, and RNA editing.

### Plastid vs. Mitochondrial Genome

| Feature | Plastid Genome | Mitochondrial Genome |
|---|---|---|
| Cellular location | Chloroplast/plastid | Mitochondrion |
| Main biological functions | Photosynthesis and other plastid functions | Energy-related mitochondrial functions |
| Organization | Relatively conserved; *D. officinale* plastid genome is circular | Can be multichromosomal and structurally variable |
| Genome size | 152,221 bp for *D. officinale* NC_024019.1 | OR413912.1 is 65,398 bp, but represents mitochondrial chromosome 1 only |
| Gene content | Contains protein-coding, tRNA, and rRNA genes | Contains protein-coding, tRNA, and rRNA genes |
| Copy number | Not determined in this activity | Not determined in this activity |
| Inheritance | Not determined from the data used | Not determined from the data used |
| Structural change | Relatively conserved, with variation around IR/SSC regions | Greater structural variation and rearrangement reported in *Dendrobium* |
| RNA editing | RNA editing reported in chloroplast genes | RNA editing reported in mitochondrial genes |
| Research applications | Phylogenetics, species identification, molecular markers, and evolutionary studies | Comparative genomics, genome evolution, phylogenetics, and mitochondrial studies |

## 10. Practical Value of Plastid Genomes

Complete plastid genomes are useful for studying plant evolution and relationships among species. They can also be used for species identification and for developing molecular markers. Luo et al. (2014) demonstrated that complete chloroplast genomes can provide useful information for phylogenetic studies of Orchidaceae.

One advantage of using plastid genomes is that they contain many genes and structural features that can be compared among plant species. Complete plastid genomes can therefore provide more information for phylogenetic analysis than examining only one short DNA region.

However, plastid genomes represent only one part of the genetic information of a plant. They cannot replace nuclear genomic data when the research question concerns nuclear genes or nuclear genetic variation.

A research question suitable for plastid genomes would be:

> **How are different *Dendrobium* species related based on their complete chloroplast genomes?**

A research question better suited to nuclear genomic data would be:

> **Which nuclear genomic regions are associated with a particular inherited trait in *Dendrobium*?**

## Conclusion

The complete chloroplast genome of *Dendrobium officinale* was characterized using NCBI RefSeq information and Galaxy sequence statistics. The genome, accession **NC_024019.1**, is a circular chloroplast genome with a length of **152,221 bp**. Galaxy analysis produced one sequence with no gaps and an overall GC content of **37.47%**.

The genome contains protein-coding genes, tRNA genes, and rRNA genes and is organized into LSC, SSC, and two inverted repeat regions. Important features include duplicated genes in the IR regions, RNA-related genes, the trnK intron containing matK, and RNA-editing events reported in the literature.

Comparison with *Dendrobium* mitochondrial genomes shows that both organelles contain their own genetic material and share several types of genomic features, but mitochondrial genomes can display greater structural variation and multichromosomal organization. Overall, the characterization of the *D. officinale* plastid genome demonstrates how complete plastid sequences can be used to study genome organization, gene content, plant evolution, and relationships among species.

